.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt BRK 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm brk ,
.Nm mmap2 ,
.Nm munmap ,
.Nm mprotect
.Nd change the heap, anonymous memory mappings and their protection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd "#define _BSD_SOURCE"
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn brk "void *addr"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall "long number" "..."
.In sys/syscall.h
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft void *
.Fn mmap "void *addr" "size_t length" "int prot" "int flags" "int fd" "off_t offset"
.Ft int
.Fn munmap "void *addr" "size_t length"
.Ft int
.Fn mprotect "void *addr" "size_t length" "int prot"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These kernel calls change the process's virtual memory areas (VMAs).
Pages are 4096 bytes.
Establishing a heap range or an anonymous mapping reserves virtual
addresses; physical frames are allocated and zeroed on first access.
Success does not guarantee that a later fault can obtain a frame.
An unresolved user-mode fault terminates the process with
.Dv SIGSEGV .
.Ss Program break
The raw
.Fn brk
call, number 45, takes the requested break in EBX and returns the
resulting break.
A zero request queries the current break.
The heap is the range from the heap base to the break rounded up to a
page boundary.
Growth adds a read-write VMA above the old top, which merges with a
read-write VMA below it; shrinkage removes the range above the new
rounded boundary and releases its pages.
.Fn mprotect
may divide the heap into VMAs of different protection; growth and
shrinkage work on the range all the same.
.Pp
A request below the heap base, above the stack limit, overlapping
another area, exceeding
.Dv RLIMIT_AS ,
or requiring a VMA that cannot be allocated leaves the break unchanged.
The break itself need not be page-aligned.
.Pp
Musl's public
.Fn brk
function always fails with
.Er ENOMEM .
It does not issue kernel call 45.
The kernel interface is available through
.Fn syscall
with
.Dv SYS_brk .
.Ss Anonymous mappings
Musl exposes
.Fn mmap ,
which uses kernel call 192,
.Nm mmap2 .
It has no public function named
.Fn mmap2 .
The raw call takes
.Fa addr ,
.Fa length ,
.Fa prot ,
.Fa flags ,
.Fa fd
and a page offset in EBX, ECX, EDX, ESI, EDI and EBP respectively.
The offset in the public
.Fn mmap
interface is in bytes; musl divides it by 4096.
.Pp
The kernel requires
.Dv MAP_ANONYMOUS ;
file mappings are not implemented, and the file descriptor and page
offset are ignored.
The mapping type in the low four bits must be
.Dv MAP_SHARED ,
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE
or
.Dv MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE .
Every accepted mapping has private copy-on-write behavior across fork,
.Dv MAP_SHARED
included.
Other flag bits are ignored.
.Pp
.Fa length
is rounded up to a page boundary.
With
.Dv MAP_FIXED ,
the mapping is placed at the page-aligned
.Fa addr
and replaces whatever that range held; with
.Dv MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE ,
a range already in use is refused.
Otherwise a page-aligned
.Fa addr
whose range is free and ends below the stack's growth limit is used as
it is, and without such a hint the kernel takes the highest free range
below
.Ql 0xB0000000
and above the heap's top, so the ranges
.Fn munmap
frees are used again.
.Pp
.Fa prot
uses
.Dv PROT_READ ,
.Dv PROT_WRITE
and
.Dv PROT_EXEC .
Write permission also grants read permission on this i386 kernel.
.Dv PROT_NONE
(zero) denies access.
Execute permission also grants read permission and is not checked
separately from it on a fault.
Other protection bits are refused.
.Ss Unmapping
.Fn munmap ,
kernel call 91, removes the range beginning at the page-aligned
.Fa addr .
.Fa length
must be nonzero; the end is rounded up to a page boundary.
The range must not wrap or extend beyond
.Ql 0xC0000000 .
The call removes, trims or splits overlapping VMAs and frees their
resident frames in the range.
Unmapped pages and pages never accessed require no frame release.
.Ss Protection
.Fn mprotect ,
kernel call 125, gives the range beginning at the page-aligned
.Fa addr
the protection
.Fa prot ,
with the bits and meanings
.Fn mmap
uses.
The end is rounded up to a page boundary, and a zero
.Fa length
changes nothing.
Every page of the range must belong to a VMA.
The call splits the VMAs at the ends of the range, sets the protection
of the pieces inside, merges pieces that now match their neighbors,
and then changes the pages already resident.
A page made inaccessible keeps its frame and contents.
Write permission is granted in place on a page the process alone
holds; a page it shares with a forked relative becomes copy-on-write,
so the first write copies it.
A read-only VMA refuses writes even to such a page.
.Ss Limits
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_AS
limits the total bytes described by the process's VMAs.
The VMA array grows as needed to at most
.Dv VMA_LIMIT
(65536) entries, including the program, heap and stack areas.
Splitting an area for
.Fn munmap
can require an additional entry, and
.Fn mprotect
up to two.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The raw
.Fn brk
call returns the resulting break; a refused change normally returns
the old break, not a negative error number.
Musl's
.Fn brk
returns -1 and sets
.Va errno
to
.Er ENOMEM .
.Pp
.Fn mmap
returns the mapped address, or
.Dv MAP_FAILED
and sets
.Va errno .
.Fn munmap
and
.Fn mprotect
return 0, or -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw mapping calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/vmprobe.c
queries the raw break, grows it by four pages, checks zero-filled
memory and writes, then restores the break.
It also maps eight anonymous read-write pages, checks their contents,
and unmaps them.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
An unsuccessful VMA insertion by
.Nm mmap2
or split by
.Fn munmap
or
.Fn mprotect
reports once per process:
.Dl vm: OPERATION: pid PID (NAME) has used all N of its mappings
This message can also result from failure to allocate VMA storage,
rather than reaching the count limit.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENOMEM
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE
names a range in use.
.It Er EINVAL
For
.Fn mmap ,
.Fa length
is zero, the mapping type is not one of the three above, or
.Fa prot
has an unknown bit;
with
.Dv MAP_FIXED ,
.Fa addr
is not page-aligned.
For
.Fn mprotect ,
.Fa addr
is not page-aligned or
.Fa prot
has an unknown bit.
For
.Fn munmap ,
.Fa length
is zero,
the address is not page-aligned, is at or above
.Ql 0xC0000000 ,
or the range extends past that address.
Musl also rejects an
.Fn mmap
offset that is negative, not page-aligned, or not representable as the
kernel's page offset.
.It Er ENOMEM
An anonymous mapping has no suitable address range, would exceed
.Dv RLIMIT_AS ,
or cannot obtain a VMA entry, or a fixed mapping lies outside user
space.
Unmapping cannot obtain an entry needed to split an area.
A page of an
.Fn mprotect
range belongs to no VMA, or the range lies outside user space, or the
split cannot obtain its entries.
Musl also rejects mapping lengths at least
.Dv PTRDIFF_MAX
and every public
.Fn brk
request.
.It Er ENOSYS
A mapping lacks
.Dv MAP_ANONYMOUS .
.It Er ESRCH
The kernel has no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr memory 7
